  ],
  "claims": [
    {
      "claim": "All transferable L1 gold-voting records report human absolute oral bioavailability at or above 46% (alprazolam 80–100% and SR 84–91%; estazolam 87%; brigatinib 46%; GSK1322322 57–77%; suvorexant 82%; clobazam 90%; ranitidine 54–58%; 2,4-D rapid/complete), so none provides affirmative evidence for F < 20%.",
      "card_ids": ["C01","C02","C03","C04","C05","C06","C07","C08","C09","C10","C11","C12","C13","C14","C15","C16","C17"]
    },
    {
      "claim": "L2 context records are predominantly supportive (estazolam ~98–100% in mice; alprazolam XR 92%; ranitidine in cirrhosis 70%; 2,4-D virtually 100%; brigatinib orally bioavailable).",
      "card_ids": ["C18","C20","C21","C22","C23","C24","C25","C26","C27","C28","C29"]
    },
    {
      "claim": "L4 analog absorption/solubility/permeability evidence is supportive: chloroquine is highly absorbed and freely soluble; amodiaquine HCl equilibrium solubility is 6.9–8.9 mg/mL across pH 1.0–6.4; midazolam shows high Caco-2 Papp (6.99×10^-5 cm/s).",
      "card_ids": ["C42","C44","C45","C46","C47","C48","C49","C50","C51","C52","C53"]
    },
    {
      "claim": "L5 amodiaquine gut-wall evidence shows high Caco-2 permeability (~20×10^-6 cm/s), higher apical-to-basolateral transport than mefloquine, and only limited/saturable P-gp efflux (ER 1.3 at 10 µM), supporting no major gut-wall barrier for this 4-aminoquinoline chemotype.",
      "card_ids": ["C54","C56","C58","C60","C62"]
    },
    {
      "claim": "L6 amodiaquine hepatic-clearance records show high intrinsic clearance and predicted high hepatic extraction (HLM CLint 440 µL/min/mg; CLint 120.6 µL/min/mg for DEAQ formation; HLM t1/2 15.8 min; predicted CLH 19.1 mL/min/kg; classified >70% liver blood flow), indicating a potential first-pass liability for the closest analog chemotype; however these are analog, not query, measurements and do not establish F < 20%.",
      "card_ids": ["C67","C68","C69","C72","C74","C75","C76"]
    },
    {
      "claim": "The only low-F record (alprazolam in rat B, F=0.045 at 12.5 mg/kg) is species- and dose-specific and does not provide affirmative threshold-relevant evidence against the query.",
      "card_ids": ["C19"]
    }
  ],
  "new_evidence_assessment": [
    {
      "family": "hepatic_clearance_metabolic_stability",
      "applicability": "moderate",
      "direction": "contradictory",
      "decision_effect": "weakened",
      "card_ids": ["C67","C68","C69","C72","C74","C75","C76"]
    }
  ],
  "evidence_gaps": [
    "No direct query-specific absolute oral bioavailability or matched oral/IV AUC measurement is available.",
    "No query-specific hepatic clearance or metabolic-stability measurement; L6 records are for amodiaquine/DEAQ, and the query differs by a benzylamine linker and lacks the phenolic OH.",
    "No query-specific CYP metabolism data; the dimethylaminomethyl side chain may be metabolized differently from amodiaquine's diethylaminomethyl phenol.",
    "Exact dose, formulation, prandial state, and species are unspecified.",
    "The query is highly ionized at physiological pH (neutral fraction 0.01), though logD 2.15 and low TPSA support adequate passive permeability; no direct query permeability measurement is available.",
    "High hepatic clearance of amodiaquine does not by itself quantify F relative to the 20% threshold."
  ],
  "decision_summary": "Predicted high (F >= 20%) because all transferable L1 gold outcomes are >=46%, L2/L4/L5 context is supportive, and the physicochemical prior is favorable; L6 amodiaquine hepatic-clearance records introduce a first-pass liability for the closest chemotype but are analog-only and do not affirmatively establish F < 20%, so the prediction is retained but weakened."
}

